---
import ContentLayout from '@layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '@config/site';
---

<ContentLayout
  title="How We Build Our Calculators"
  description={`Our editorial and engineering process: how ${SITE.name} researches formulas, tests for accuracy, and reviews every tool before it goes live.`}
  path="/methodology"
  heading="How we build our calculators"
  updated="2026-07-17"
>
  <p>
    A calculator is only worth using if you can trust its answer. This page explains
    the process behind every tool on {SITE.name} so you can judge our work for
    yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Research the formula</h2>
  <p>
    We start from authoritative, primary sources — for example, the World Health
    Organization's classifications for health metrics, and standard financial
    mathematics for loans and interest. We document the exact formula each calculator
    uses in its on-page explainer.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Implement it transparently</h2>
  <p>
    The calculation logic is written as small, pure functions kept separate from the
    interface. This makes the maths easy to audit and reuse, and keeps the tools fast
    because the heavy lifting happens instantly in your browser.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Test for correctness</h2>
  <p>
    Each formula is covered by an automated test suite that checks it against known
    values and edge cases — zero-interest loans, boundary BMI categories, order of
    operations, and more. A calculator does not ship until its tests pass.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Review before launch</h2>
  <p>
    Before a tool goes live it is reviewed for accuracy, clarity and accessibility.
    Calculator pages display the date their method was last reviewed so you know how
    current the information is.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Maintain and correct</h2>
  <p>
    Rules and rates change. When they do — or when a reader flags an issue — we update
    the tool and its explainer. If you spot something that looks wrong, please
    <a href="/contact">tell us</a>; corrections are a priority.
  </p>

  <h2>Important limitations</h2>
  <p>
    Our calculators are educational tools, not professional advice. Financial, health
    and legal decisions depend on individual circumstances that a general calculator
    cannot capture. Always consult a qualified professional before making important
    decisions. See our <a href="/disclaimer">disclaimer</a> for details.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
